WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.123
"Laurie, come take a look at this."

2
00:00:07.124 --> 00:00:10.815
Grandpa was reading the newspaper at his desk.

3
00:00:10.816 --> 00:00:16.507
"There's going to be a Renaissance fair in Buck County. Do you want to go?"

4
00:00:16.508 --> 00:00:21.166
"What's a Renaissance fair?" Laurie asked, looking up from her book.

5
00:00:21.167 --> 00:00:34.220
"A Renaissance fair," Grandpa began reading from the newspaper ad, "is a place to see, hear, and taste life as it was during Elizabethan England."

6
00:00:34.221 --> 00:00:43.389
He looked at Laurie. "That means the time when Queen Elizabeth was on the throne, from 1558 to 1603.

7
00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:46.826
It was the height of the English Renaissance.

8
00:00:46.827 --> 00:00:51.497
There will probably be a market, performances, and actors."

9
00:00:51.498 --> 00:00:55.351
"What does renaissance mean?" Laurie asked.

10
00:00:55.352 --> 00:01:05.204
"Aren't you taking French at school?" Grandpa said teasingly. "Renaissance means rebirth in French.

11
00:01:05.205 --> 00:01:12.414
We call the period of European history from the fourteenth to the sixteenth century the Renaissance

12
00:01:12.415 --> 00:01:17.368
because all of Europe changed dramatically during this time.

13
00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:22.556
There was a rebirth in art, education, government, and religion.

14
00:01:22.557 --> 00:01:28.929
It was also a time of great discovery in both the sciences and geography. "The

15
00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:34.218
Renaissance began in Florence, Italy, in the 1300s. Florence

16
00:01:34.219 --> 00:01:42.144
was a big trading city because it was located neatly between the Middle East, Europe, and Asia. Many

17
00:01:42.145 --> 00:01:47.246
merchants, who used to be part of the lower class, became wealthy. This

18
00:01:47.247 --> 00:01:50.694
was the beginning of the middle class. They

19
00:01:50.695 --> 00:01:58.837
weren't nobles or royalty, and they didn't have any fancy titles to their names, but they had money to spend freely. They

20
00:01:58.838 --> 00:02:03.278
also had free time to think, read, and enjoy life. Many

21
00:02:03.279 --> 00:02:09.253
scholars think this brought about a renewed interest in art and learning. "The

22
00:02:09.254 --> 00:02:14.473
middle class wanted to spend their wealth on art, books, and music. Wealthy

23
00:02:14.474 --> 00:02:22.067
families gave money to artists and asked them to make paintings and sculptures for the city. Many

24
00:02:22.068 --> 00:02:25.954
of these artists are still admired today. Let's

25
00:02:25.955 --> 00:02:30.493
see, I'm sure I have a book around here somewhere . . ." Grandpa

26
00:02:30.494 --> 00:02:33.942
stood up and examined his bookshelf. "Aha!

27
00:02:33.943 --> 00:02:40.159
Artists of the Italian Renaissance ," he read, pulling the title from the shelf.

28
00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:44.206
"I'm sure you'll recognize a lot of stuff in here."

29
00:02:44.207 --> 00:02:50.310
Laurie flipped through the pages. "The Mona Lisa !" she

30
00:02:50.311 --> 00:02:57.157
exclaimed. "Right! The Mona Lisa was painted by Leonardo da Vinci in the early

31
00:02:57.158 --> 00:03:03.266
1500s." Laurie turned the page. "Oh! He's naked!" she

32
00:03:03.267 --> 00:03:08.955
cried. "That's Michelangelo's famous sculpture David ,"

33
00:03:08.956 --> 00:03:15.761
Grandpa answered. "During the Renaissance, artists began learning how to create more realistic images

34
00:03:15.762 --> 00:03:22.276
of humans. They studied the human body carefully, and tried to draw and sculpt what

35
00:03:22.277 --> 00:03:25.588
they saw. It was really a revolution

36
00:03:25.589 --> 00:03:31.646
in art. "Education in Europe also developed and changed during

37
00:03:31.647 --> 00:03:37.658
the Renaissance. In the Middle Ages, people learned to read and write so they could study and copy

38
00:03:37.659 --> 00:03:42.566
the Bible. But people of the Renaissance wanted to study books from classical Greece

39
00:03:42.567 --> 00:03:46.429
and Rome. This was called new learning

40
00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:54.190
or humanism." "Humanism?" Laurie echoed. "That's a funny word. Were they

41
00:03:54.191 --> 00:04:04.004
studying humans?" "Well, yes!" Grandpa answered. "People were curious about what human beings could do with science, art,

42
00:04:04.005 --> 00:04:10.922
philosophy—everything! They believed that humans had skills, and that these skills could be developed to

43
00:04:10.923 --> 00:04:14.716
perfection. What's one of your skills, Laurie?" Grandpa

44
00:04:14.717 --> 00:04:20.145
asked. "Um, I always get As in reading," Laurie

45
00:04:20.146 --> 00:04:27.269
replied. "So you would have been encouraged to develop that talent by reading classic Greek and Roman

46
00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:32.508
books. Unfortunately this would only have been encouraged if you were a

47
00:04:32.509 --> 00:04:38.774
boy. Girls received just basic education and then learned to cook and sew at

48
00:04:38.775 --> 00:04:48.572
home. Rather than going to a university at age 15, like boys did, girls would probably get married to an older man and start a

49
00:04:48.573 --> 00:04:52.193
family." "That's not fair." Laurie

50
00:04:52.194 --> 00:05:01.155
frowned. "No, it's not. But many things came out of the Renaissance that everybody, including women, could appreciate

51
00:05:01.156 --> 00:05:08.274
later. One of the greatest inventions was the printing press, which was created in Germany in

52
00:05:08.275 --> 00:05:13.806
1440. Thanks to the printing press, books didn't have to be copied by hand

53
00:05:13.807 --> 00:05:17.817
anymore. So they were cheaper and more people could buy

54
00:05:17.818 --> 00:05:25.182
them. Libraries were built and regular people, not just nobles or clergymen, learned how to

55
00:05:25.183 --> 00:05:32.256
read. The printing press changed European—no, human—society

56
00:05:32.257 --> 00:05:35.735
forever! Can you imagine a world without books,

57
00:05:35.736 --> 00:05:40.852
Laurie?" Laurie shook her head forcefully. "No

58
00:05:40.853 --> 00:05:44.195
way!" "Many things were invented during the

59
00:05:44.196 --> 00:05:48.556
Renaissance. Have you heard of the Italian Galileo

60
00:05:48.557 --> 00:05:55.447
Galilei? Human eyes saw the surface of the moon for the first time with his

61
00:05:55.448 --> 00:06:04.187
telescope. He was also influential in getting people to accept the idea that the Earth moves around the sun, and not the other way

62
00:06:04.188 --> 00:06:06.948
around." "Of course the Earth moves around the

63
00:06:06.949 --> 00:06:12.952
sun! Even I know that and I'm only in fourth grade." Laurie

64
00:06:12.953 --> 00:06:17.927
giggled. "Will we see Galileo's telescope at the fair?" she

65
00:06:17.928 --> 00:06:24.055
asked. "No. Galileo was Italian and we're going to an English fair,

66
00:06:24.056 --> 00:06:31.601
remember? The Renaissance began in Italy, but later it spread to England, France, Germany, and all of

67
00:06:31.602 --> 00:06:40.082
Europe. We'll probably see actors playing famous people of the English Renaissance, like the writer William Shakespeare.

68
00:06:40.083 --> 00:06:47.476
Or Sir John Harington, inventor of the flushing toilet—another great invention of the Renaissance!

69
00:06:47.477 --> 00:06:53.919
And at the market I bet we'll see barrel makers, blacksmiths, and milliners."

70
00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:57.810
"Millionaires!" Laurie said, surprised.

71
00:06:57.811 --> 00:07:05.151
"No—milliners! They make hats," Grandpa answered with a chuckle.

72
00:07:05.152 --> 00:07:08.477
"Hats and wigs were in fashion back then.

73
00:07:08.478 --> 00:07:14.171
In fact, anyone over age 12 was required to wear a hat.

74
00:07:14.172 --> 00:07:20.674
During the Renaissance, clothing was a symbol of wealth and class—by law.

75
00:07:20.675 --> 00:07:27.114
For example, nobody except the queen was allowed to wear purple.

76
00:07:27.115 --> 00:07:32.085
And peasants weren't allowed to wear pink or gold-colored fabric!"

77
00:07:32.086 --> 00:07:37.931
"That's crazy!" Laurie cried. She looked down at her own clothes.

78
00:07:37.932 --> 00:07:40.713
"What are we going to wear to the fair?"

79
00:07:40.714 --> 00:07:44.841
"Well, we could go in our regular clothes.

80
00:07:44.842 --> 00:07:47.996
But it would be fun to get dressed up, wouldn't it?"

81
00:07:47.997 --> 00:07:55.056
Grandpa grinned. He grabbed a long black feather from his desk and stuck it behind her ear.

82
00:07:55.058 --> 00:08:06.166
Laurie's eyes shined. "Yeah, it would! I can't wait."

